  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the worm in a worm gear?",
      "a": "The worm is a shaft with a spiral thread, like a screw. When it spins, the thread slides along the teeth of the worm wheel and pushes them round.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is it called a worm?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a worm gear slow things down so much?",
      "a": "A single-thread worm moves the wheel on by only one tooth per turn. So the gear ratio equals the number of teeth on the wheel, such as 40 to 1, far more than two ordinary gears give.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the gear ratio of a worm gear?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why can the wheel not turn the worm back?",
      "a": "Pushing on the wheel presses its teeth against the shallow thread of the worm, and friction is too big for the thread to slide. So most worm gears lock, which keeps loads from slipping back.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a self-locking gear?",
        "Why do worm gears not run backwards?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where are worm gears used?",
      "a": "Worm gears are used where you need a big slow-down or a gear that holds its position: guitar tuning pegs, winches, lifts, gates and speedometers.",
      "asks": [
        "What uses a worm gear?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Are worm gears efficient?",
      "a": "Less than ordinary gears. The thread slides against the teeth instead of rolling, so more energy turns into heat. That same friction is what makes them self-locking.",
      "asks": [
        "Do worm gears waste energy?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A single-thread worm moves its wheel on by one tooth per turn.",
    "Guitar tuning pegs use small worm gears so strings stay in tune under tension."
  ],
